    "sun": "succession is the one problem the eldest cannot hand anywhere. there is no board above you whose decision it formally is, no predecessor to ring, and the date that made you senior is the same date that made the question yours alone. so eleven years of it have been conducted inside one head, with both candidates working hard in front of you, reading your attention as a verdict already reached.",
    "moon": "from inside, no delay has occurred. both of them are right for it in different directions, and holding two whole answers has never felt like failing to pick — the eleven years were spent building both, on purpose, in earnest, which is why neither has a gap you could decide on. nothing in you has ever produced the discomfort that makes other people settle it early.",
    "pull": "you have two successors because you wanted two, and the post takes one, and the day you say a name the other leaves with fifteen years in them and every reason to.",
    "tension": "the building has known for years and picked its own favourite, so whichever name you say confirms a story written without you. the one who goes will be described as having taken it badly, by people who were promised nothing themselves. what cannot be shown to either of them is the account in which both were true at once — it was never written down anywhere.",
    "cost": "the place has two people ready instead of one, which almost nowhere manages, and it survives whatever happens to you because of that. the second of them is being spent to produce it. eleven years of a life have gone into a run that had one finishing place, and the one who could have stopped it earlier is the person who wanted both."
