    "sun": "getting there first is supposed to end in a handover — you hold the thing until whoever it belongs to turns up. no part of you argues for the handover. there is no prior claim to cite, no arrangement it would disturb, so the matter settles where it landed and acquires your name by sitting there, and the people who arrive later find it already looking like yours.",
    "moon": "and the speed does not slow down for what is already installed. you go on getting there first, every few weeks, into rooms whose trouble has nowhere else to be put afterwards. so the count rises. each night you were quickest to reach leaves a resident behind, and the next call comes to you faster than the last, because by then the address is known.",
    "pull": "first at everything, owner of all of it. nobody handed you a single one, and nobody can remember a time when they were not yours.",
    "tension": "you volunteer for everything, they say, usually kindly, sometimes as a diagnosis. no volunteering has occurred. you were nearest the door and had nothing waiting on the other side of it, and the two facts got read as a decision you made about your own life. arguing it means describing an absence, which sounds like modesty from outside and gets waved off as such, so the account holds and more arrives under it.",
    "cost": "an emergency at three in the morning meets no obstacle in you — nothing to move first, no arrangement to protect, no calculation about the week. it goes straight in. what goes in stays at the size it arrived, with no date on it, so returning any of it later requires proving it was somebody's before it was yours, and the proof is what an empty place cannot keep."
